## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to model constraints with DP, including exact count, non-adjacency, and reconstruction; this is a common interview pattern for medium-difficulty tasks.

## Key Concepts

- 2D DP with adjacency constraint
- Exact-count optimization
- Backtracking to recover indices

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxPromo(revenue, k, cost){
  const n = revenue.length;
  const NEG = Number.NEGATIVE_INFINITY;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++){
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++){
      // skip i
      dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i][t], dp[i-1][t]);
      // take i
      if (t > 0){
        const takeFrom = i >= 2 ? dp[i-2][t-1] : (t-1 === 0 ? 0 : NEG);
        const val = takeFrom + revenue[i-1] - cost;
        dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i][t], val);
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction
  let i = n, t = k;
  const picks = [];
  while (i > 0 && t >= 0){
    if (dp[i][t] === dp[i-1][t]){ i--; }
    else { picks.unshift(i); i -= 2; t--; }
  }
  return { best: dp[n][k], picks };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you optimize space to O(k)?
- How to handle ties in reconstruction?

## Why This Question Is Asked

This problem tests dynamic programming with turn constraints on grid paths, requiring careful state design and path reconstruction. It evaluates understanding of edge case handling and performance optimization for large inputs.

## Key Concepts

- **4D DP state**: position (i, j), turn count (t), and ending direction (d)
- **Two transition types**: continuing in the same direction versus making a turn
- **Path reconstruction**: using parent pointers or backtracking to retrieve the actual path
- **Time and space trade-offs**: consider optimization for large grids

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxPathWithKTurns(M, k) {
  const n = M.length;
  const m = M[0].length;
  const dp = Array(n).fill(null).map(() =>
    Array(m).fill(null).map(() =>
      Array(k + 1).fill(null).map(() => [-1, -1])
    )
  );

  dp[0][0][0][0] = dp[0][0][0][1] = M[0][0];

  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < m; j++) {
      for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
        for (let d = 0; d < 2; d++) {
          if (dp[i][j][t][d] < 0) continue;

          if (i + 1 < n) {
            const newD = 1;
            const newT = d === newD ? t : t + 1;
            if (newT <= k) {
              dp[i + 1][j][newT][newD] = Math.max(
                dp[i + 1][j][newT][newD],
                dp[i][j][t][d] + M[i + 1][j]
              );
            }
          }

          if (j + 1 < m) {
            const newD = 0;
            const newT = d === newD ? t : t + 1;
            if (newT <= k) {
              dp[i][j + 1][newT][newD] = Math.max(
                dp[i][j + 1][newT][newD],
                dp[i][j][t][d] + M[i][j + 1]
              );
            }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  return Math.max(dp[n - 1][m - 1][k][0], dp[n - 1][m - 1][k][1]);
}
```

## Complexity

- **Time**: O(n × m × k)
- **Space**: O(n × m × k)

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to combine subarray maxima with partition DP, requiring both maximization and reconstruction of multiple intervals. It exercises careful indexing and boundary handling.

## Key Concepts
- Non-overlapping subarrays with a cap k
- Precomputation of maxSub for O(1) subarray maxima
- 2D DP with reconstruction pointers
- Time/space tradeoffs and edge cases (k=0, equal values)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxValueWithKSubarrays(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const maxSub = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(0));
  for (let l = 0; l < n; l++) {
    let m = -Infinity;
    for (let r = l; r < n; r++) {
      m = Math.max(m, A[r]);
      maxSub[l][r] = m;
    }
  }
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(0));
  const take = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      let best = dp[i-1][t];
      let bestJ = -1;
      for (let j = 1; j <= i; j++) {
        const val = maxSub[j-1][i-1] + dp[j-1][t-1];
        if (val > best) { best = val; bestJ = j; }
      }
      dp[i][t] = best; take[i][t] = bestJ;
    }
  }
  const res = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (t > 0 && i > 0) {
    const j = take[i][t];
    if (j === -1) break;
    res.push([j-1, i-1]);
    i = j - 1;
    t--;
  }
  res.reverse();
  return {value: dp[n][k], subarrays: res};
}
```

